\section{What it assumes, and why one argument has no default}{


\code{\link[=kp.degree.estimator]{kp.degree.estimator()}} returns each respondent's degree with respect to the
\strong{whole population}: how many people they know, full stop. Visibility is a
narrower thing --- how many \emph{frame-population members} could report an alter.
Converting one into the other needs the share of the population that is in
the frame, and nothing in the data supplies it. That is \code{frame.ratio}, and it
deliberately has no default, for the same reason \code{\link[=tie_config]{tie_config()}} has no
default structure: getting it wrong scales every estimate by a constant and
nothing complains.

Two further assumptions come with the method rather than with this function,
and are recorded in the provenance:
\itemize{
\item the tie is roughly symmetric, so that an alter's connections to frame
members can be inferred from respondents' connections in general;
\item respondents' degrees stand in for alters'. Where the two populations
differ --- and for mortality they differ in the most relevant way, since
the alters include the dead --- this is the same substitution
\code{\link[=vis_from_donor]{vis_from_donor()}} makes, with the same direction of error.
}
